# THE UNSERVED SHARES OF A LAYER, TOTALLED, WITH WHAT WOULD SUSPEND THE TOTAL. One row per layer
# holding at least one `customer` or `unrealised` share: how many such holders, how many left
# their share unstated, the sum of the ones that stated it, and the units they used.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ TWO CALLERS COMPUTED THIS γ SEPARATELY AND DISAGREED ABOUT ITS POPULATION.
#    arithmetic/exposure_bounded.sqlc inner-joins entries/unserved_holders.sqlc, so a layer with
#    no unserved holder is not a candidate. checks/exposure_unaccounted.sqlc outer-joined it and
#    supplied `coalesce(unserved, 0)`, so six such layers entered its examined set carrying a
#    zero nobody filed. All six read `exposure = 0`, so the check could not fire on any of them
#    and still counted them toward its coverage: 9 examined against a site that declares 3.
#
# ⛔⛔ AN UNSTATED SHARE IS NOT A ZERO SHARE, WHICH IS WHY `unstated` IS A COLUMN AND NOT A
#    FILTER. `sum()` skips NULLs silently, so a layer where nobody stated a share sums to NULL
#    and one where half did sums to half the truth. Both callers must decide what to do about
#    that, and they decide differently: the site reports `suspended`, the check declines to
#    examine. Carrying the count lets each say so instead of each rediscovering it.
#
# ⭐ `share_units` IS THE DISTINCT SET, so a caller comparing against its layer's own unit asks
#   whether that set is exactly its own unit and nothing else. A NULL unit lands in the array and
#   makes the comparison false, which is correct: an unstated unit is not a matching one.
-- entries/unserved_holders.sqlc folded to one row per layer.
SELECT h.filing, h.layer,
       count(*)                                        AS holders,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE h.share_high IS NULL)     AS unstated,
       sum(h.share_high)                                AS unserved_high,
       sum(h.share_mode)                                AS unserved_mode,
       array_agg(DISTINCT h.share_unit)                 AS share_units
FROM (
    :compose(entries/unserved_holders.sqlc)
) h
GROUP BY h.filing, h.layer
